# WHICH CHAIN THIS ESTATE IS ON. EMBER MAINNET, `hearth`, CHAIN ID 7411.
#
# Selected by `CF_EMBER_NETWORK` — `docker-compose.estate.yml` loads
# `env/chain.${CF_EMBER_NETWORK:-mainnet}.env` into `indexer` and `foresight`, so
# exactly one of this file and `chain.testnet.env` is ever read, and no deploy can
# have half of each.
#
# ── WHY A FILE AND NOT SIX MORE LINES IN THE COMPOSE FILE ────────────────────
#
# Two of the variables below carry the network IN THEIR NAME
# (`INDEXER_RPC_EMBER_MAINNET`), and a compose `environment:` KEY cannot be
# interpolated — only a value can. An `env_file:` PATH can be, and Compose v5.1.1
# interpolates the values inside one as well (measured on the deployment host, not
# assumed), so `${EMBER_RPC_URL}` below still resolves at compose time.
#
# Adding the mainnet variable beside the testnet one and letting the unused half
# sit there is not an option either: `indexer/src/env.ts:351` refuses to boot when
# an `INDEXER_RPC_*` names a scope `INDEXER_CHAINS` does not list.
#
# ── THE DEFECT THIS FILE EXISTS TO CLOSE ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both estates ran chain id 7412. `hearth/node/src/params.js:37-38` maps
# `hearth` to 7411 and `hearth-testnet` to 7412, and BOTH seeds were started as
# `hearth-testnet` — two chains with separate volumes, separate history and no
# peering (8646 is unpublished on both), sharing one identity. Nothing merges
# them, but a transaction signed for one is REPLAYABLE on the other, because
# EIP-155 binds a signature to the chain id and nothing else.
#
# `docker-compose.hearth-seed.yml` now runs this environment's seed as network
# `hearth`, and `docker-compose.miners.yml` mines it as `hearth`.

# ── THE INDEXER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `indexer/src/evm.ts:471-480` reads `eth_chainId` before it writes a block and
# refuses anything that is not `declaredChainId(chain, network)` —
# `contracts/packages/chain/src/index.ts:199` pins EMBER at
# `{ mainnet: 7411, testnet: 7412 }`. So `ember:mainnet` here IS the assertion
# that the node is 7411; there is no second place to state it and no way to be
# wrong quietly.
# ── ONE POD INDEXES BOTH ESTATES' CHAINS ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# `docs/network-consolidation.md`. The indexer keeps ONE database with a
# `network` column (§5.4), so there is no second pod to run the testnet chains —
# this list is the whole estate's chain set, not mainnet's.
#
# It was mainnet-only from the cutover until 2026-08-25, and nothing said so.
# The testnet chain configuration sat in `chain.testnet.env`, which after the
# cutover no pod mounted, so testnet EMBER and LTC were simply not indexed. What
# hid it was `prometheus.yml`'s `cf-indexer-testnet` job: its target
# `indexer.cf-testnet…` had become a CNAME to THIS pod, so it produced
# `indexer_tip_height{estate="testnet", network="mainnet"}` — mainnet's height
# under a testnet label, moving healthily, for a chain nobody was reading.
#
# The `_TESTNET` RPC and start-height variables come from `chain.testnet.env`,
# built into secret `env-chain-testnet` beside this one; see FILES in
# scripts/k8s-secrets.py for why they are filtered to that suffix.
#
# `ltc:testnet` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. Its provider is named
# `litecoind-regtest` and no such node exists: the chain host runs exactly three
# daemons — bitcoind, litecoind and dogecoind — and all three are mainnet, with
# only :9332 listening. The last testnet LTC block indexed is 2026-08-08.
#
# Naming it cost a climbing `indexer_provider_failures_total` and a chain the
# estate claimed to index and did not, which is the dishonesty the indexer's own
# "a follower with no provider reports healthy and indexes nothing" guard exists
# to prevent. Restoring it is this one entry plus a real node.
INDEXER_CHAINS=ember:mainnet,ltc:mainnet,btc:mainnet,doge:mainnet,ember:testnet

# The provider is NAMED rather than left to derive from the host, because the
# name is the primary key of `provider_health` and the `provider` label of
# `indexer_provider_failures_total` — `host.docker.internal` as a health row
# identifies the machine, not the node.
INDEXER_RPC_EMBER_MAINNET=hearth-seed=${EMBER_RPC_URL:-http://host.docker.internal:8545}

# FROM GENESIS, and load-bearing rather than tidy. With no start height the
# follower cold-starts at `tip − 2 × depth` = `tip − 120`, which on a chain
# younger than 120 blocks does not reach back to the confirmed height — and
# `custodyTotal` then refuses with `depth_not_walked`. A chain mined from scratch
# is exactly that chain, and this one was restarted from a fresh genesis when it
# moved to 7411.
INDEXER_START_HEIGHT_EMBER_MAINNET=0

# ── FORESIGHT, AND THE GATE THAT IS BEING OPENED ON PURPOSE ──────────────────
#
# `foresight/src/env.ts:374-379` refuses to boot on `FORESIGHT_NETWORK=mainnet`
# unless `FORESIGHT_MAINNET_ENABLED=true`, so that a one-word typo cannot put
# strangers' money on a live chain. TWO deliberate settings are required, and
# deriving the second from the first would have deleted the defence — which is
# why both are written out here, in a committed file, next to each other, where
# opening the gate is a diff somebody has to read.
#
# It is opened because this EMBER "mainnet" is a private chain restarted from its
# own genesis on the deployment host, with no external holders, and because
# `FORESIGHT_TREASURY_ADDRESS`/`_ORACLE_ADDRESS` are still the zero placeholders
# that make the market contract's constructor revert — so no market can be
# created at all until an operator mints those. The day EMBER has a holder who is
# not the operator, this line is the one to revisit.
FORESIGHT_NETWORK=mainnet
FORESIGHT_MAINNET_ENABLED=true

# ── LITECOIN ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Named `litecoind=` because `parseEndpoints` takes `<name>=<url>` and that name
# is what appears in `provider_health` and in the failure metric — "litecoind"
# is the thing an operator would go and look at.
#
# The URL carries basic-auth credentials, so it lives in the gitignored
# `secrets/chainrpc.mainnet.env` and is interpolated here exactly as
# `EMBER_RPC_URL` is. `litecoind` binds RPC to the two docker bridge gateways
# only (never 0.0.0.0), with `rpcallowip` scoped to those subnets — verified
# unreachable from the LAN. The real bound is `disablewallet=1`: every container
# can reach this RPC and there are no keys behind it to use.
#
# MAINNET ONLY, deliberately. There is no Litecoin testnet node, and `env.ts`
# runs a MIRROR CHECK that refuses to boot when an `INDEXER_RPC_*` names a scope
# `INDEXER_CHAINS` does not list — so adding this to the testnet file would stop
# that estate rather than degrade it.

# ── BITCOIN ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `btc:mainnet` joins `INDEXER_CHAINS` above, and the endpoint arrives from
# `secrets/chainrpc.mainnet.env` under the name `INDEXER_RPC_BTC_MAINNET`. That
# is a RENAME and the old name is the reason this section exists.
#
# ── THE VARIABLE THAT LOOKED LIVE AND WAS READ BY NOTHING ────────────────────
#
# Until this change the secrets file carried `BTC_RPC_URL`, and the indexer had
# it in its environment on the mainnet estate — measured off the running
# container, not read out of this repository. The indexer names an endpoint
# `INDEXER_RPC_<CHAIN>_<NETWORK>` and reads no other shape, so `BTC_RPC_URL` was
# a credential-bearing URL sitting in a process that never called it. Nothing
# failed; Bitcoin was simply not indexed, and the configuration said otherwise
# to anyone reading it. micro-org#373 found it; this is the half that fixes it.
#
# The rename and the `INDEXER_CHAINS` edit above MUST land together. The mirror
# check refuses to boot when an `INDEXER_RPC_*` names a scope `INDEXER_CHAINS`
# does not list, and `resolveChains` refuses equally when a listed scope has no
# endpoint — so either half alone stops the indexer instead of degrading it, on
# a service that also follows EMBER and LTC.
#
# Named `bitcoind=` for the same reason `litecoind=` is named: the name is the
# primary key of `provider_health` and the `provider` label of
# `indexer_provider_failures_total`. Left bare it would derive from the host and
# read as `10.10.0.1:50001`, which identifies a WireGuard address rather than a
# node.
#
# ── NO START HEIGHT, AND THAT IS THE CORRECT SETTING RATHER THAN AN OMISSION ──
#
# With none set the follower cold-starts at `tip − 2 × confirmations`, and
# `contracts-chain` puts BTC at 6 — so it begins twelve blocks back, about two
# hours of chain. That window is exactly the one a deposit can be watched
# through its whole confirmation life, it costs twelve blocks of catch-up, and
# it needs no maintenance as the tip moves. EMBER pins 0 above because a chain
# restarted from its own genesis is younger than its own cold-start window; LTC
# sets none for the same reason BTC sets none. A height written here would be
# stale the day after it was written.
#
# ── THE NODE, MEASURED 2026-08-11 ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `bitcoind 27.0`, `-datadir=/data/chains/bitcoin` on the chain host, a HOST
# PROCESS and not a container. 961,940 blocks / 961,940 headers,
# `initialblockdownload: false`, `pruned: false`. `txindex` and
# `blockfilterindex` both report `synced: true` at that height — the filter
# index was still building two days ago, and the light-client differential in
# `btcdiff.ts` is what needed it. RPC is reachable from the app host over
# WireGuard only (`rpcbind=10.10.0.1`, `rpcallowip=10.10.0.2/32`), verified from
# inside `cloudsforge-estate-indexer-1` as an HTTP 401 rather than a refused
# connection.
#
# ── OBSERVING IS NOT CREDITING, AND NOTHING HERE OPENS A DEPOSIT ─────────────
#
# This line makes the indexer follow Bitcoin blocks and answer for watched
# addresses. **On its own it still does not open BTC**, and that separation is
# the point of the line rather than a caveat about it: `wallet/src/index.ts`
# wraps its observability in `payableChainsOnly`, with the payability gate
# OUTERMOST precisely so that switching a chain on here cannot open deposits as
# a side effect.
#
# The two decisions that DO open it were both taken on 2026-08-11, separately
# and in the other file, which is what the design intended:
#
#   * `SETTLEMENT_RPC_URLS` gained a `btc` key, so settlement answers `ready`
#     for the chain instead of `no_endpoint`;
#   * `WALLET_FEE_QUOTES` gained `BTC`, which is the estate's written statement
#     that it can pay a Bitcoin withdrawal — see the measurement above
#     `x-wallet-fee-quotes` in `docker-compose.estate.yml`.
#
# So this variable named three chains that were all fully open. It now names
# four, and the fourth is what turns that invariant from an assertion into a
# demonstration — see below.

# ── DOGECOIN, FOLLOWED AND DELIBERATELY NOT DEPOSITABLE ──────────────────────
#
# `doge:mainnet` joins `INDEXER_CHAINS` above and the endpoint arrives from
# `secrets/chainrpc.mainnet.env` as `INDEXER_RPC_DOGE_MAINNET`. Both halves
# landed together, for the reason the Bitcoin section states one screen up: the
# mirror check refuses to boot on either half alone.
#
# **This is the paragraph the section above used to end with, now true rather
# than hypothetical.** It said DOGE could be added here and would not become
# depositable, because it is not named in `WALLET_FEE_QUOTES`. It has been
# added, it is not in that table, and it is not depositable. The gate is
# `payableChainsOnly` in `wallet/src/index.ts`, outermost on purpose, and
# nothing in this file can reach around it.
#
# ── WHY IT IS FOLLOWED AT ALL: THE POOL RECEIVES, THE ESTATE DOES NOT PAY OUT ─
#
# `POOL_DOGE_PAYOUT_ADDRESS` is credited by AuxPoW merge-mining — Dogecoin
# blocks found as a by-product of the pool's Litecoin work. That address is real
# income arriving at an address the estate controls, and until this line existed
# nothing in the estate could see it. Following the chain is what makes a pool
# payout auditable; it is not a step toward opening DOGE deposits, and the
# decision for now is RECEIVE AND HOLD. Opening it would need a fee quote, a
# settlement endpoint and a withdrawal path, which are three separate deliberate
# edits in the other file.
#
# ── NO START HEIGHT, AND THIS ONE WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN COPIED ────────────
#
# An earlier plan for this change carried `INDEXER_START_HEIGHT_DOGE_MAINNET`
# pinned to 6,336,470 — the height merge-mining was switched on. Two
# measurements taken on 2026-08-18 removed the reason for it:
#
#   * `select count(*) from pool_blocks` is **0, for every chain** — this pool
#     has never found a block on DOGE, LTC or BTC, so there has never been a
#     payout to reach back for;
#   * the payout address reports `n_tx: 0`, `total_received: 0` (BlockCypher
#     `/v1/doge/main`, 2026-08-18) — it has never been paid by anyone else
#     either. `dogecoind 1.14.9` predates both `scantxoutset` and
#     `searchrawtransactions`, so the node itself cannot answer this and an
#     explorer is the only cheap way to ask.
#
# With nothing below the cold-start window, a pinned height buys nothing and
# costs exactly what the BITCOIN section warns of: it would be stale the day
# after it was written, and on a rebuilt database it would silently become an
# instruction to walk hundreds of thousands of blocks. DOGE credits at 30
# confirmations (`contracts/packages/chain`, derived from a measured 63.40s mean
# block time), so the cold start is `tip − 60` ≈ 63 minutes of chain, on the
# same reasoning that leaves LTC and BTC unset.
#
# The `/watch` call for the payout address carries its own `historyFromHeight`.
# That is a COMPLETENESS CLAIM and not a backfill trigger — it must be at or
# above the first block this service walked, or `custodyTotal` refuses. Since
# the address has never been paid, any such height is a true claim; the honest
# one to send is this service's own first walked DOGE height, read after it
# starts following.
#
# ── THE NODE, MEASURED 2026-08-18 ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `dogecoin 1.14.9`, `-datadir=/data/chains/dogecoin` on the chain host, a HOST
# PROCESS and not a container. Height 6,336,901, `initialblockdownload: false`,
# `pruned: false`, `txindex=1`. `getblock` verbosity 3 answers, which is better
# than this codebase's age suggests and means the worker resolves prevouts in
# one call rather than falling back to `getrawtransaction` per funding
# transaction. RPC reaches the app host over WireGuard only
# (`rpcbind=10.10.0.1`, `rpcallowip=10.10.0.2/32`).
#
# The daemon was restarted on 2026-08-18 to pick up an rpcauth rotation written
# at 03:44 that a process running since 2026-08-12 had never read. Merge-mining
# resumed with it: aux tips advancing and zero auth failures in a window
# starting strictly after the restart.
